kin, GI, constitutional symptoms)", "Obesity and tobacco use as metabolic/inflammatory risk factors"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Counter-Regulatory Hormone Excess / Paraneoplastic Endocrine Syndrome", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.45861431424512533, "posterior": 0.5498672234588289, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The absence of classic hypercortisolism signs (e.g., striae, moon face) reduces support for this diagnosis, but the rash is still consistent with paraneoplasti…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.9, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Insulin Deficiency (Beta-Cell Loss) / Autoimmune Destruction", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.035, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Without Whipple's triad, beta-cell destruction (B2) is unlikely to explain the symptoms. This finding supports alternative mechanisms."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Insulin Resistance / Metabolic Syndrome", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.14, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Severe hyperglycemia and painful rash are atypical for B3, supporting neoplastic causes like B1.1. This is weak_against for B3."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Insulin Excess / Hypoglycemia-Inducing Tumor Distractor", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.5, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Glucagonoma Syndrome (Alpha Cell Tumor)", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.24137595486585542, "posterior": 0.4647239186020099, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Severe hyperglycemia and painful rash are atypical for B3, supporting neoplastic causes like B1.1. This is weak_against for B3."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of acanthocytosis reduces support for B1.1, but multisystem symptoms maintain its possibility. This provides weak_against evidence."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.85, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Ectopic ACTH Syndrome (Hypercortisolism)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.072, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Insulinoma (Beta Cell Tumor)", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.013, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "B1", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}}, "frontier": ["B1.1", "B1.1"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Insulin Resistance / Metabolic Syndrome? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "The severe hyperglycemia and painful rash are inconsistent with insulin resistance as the primary mechanism, as these are not typical presenting features of metabolic syndrome."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of abdominal pain/diarrhea and systemic blistering-like rash align with glucagonoma syndrome (B1.1) as a paraneoplastic mechanism?", "summary": "The multisystem presentation and rash support glucagonoma syndrome (B1.1), but lack specific confirmatory features like acanthocytosis. This provides weak_for evidence."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's obesity and absence of classic paraneoplastic features strongly favor insulin resistance (B3) as the underlying mechanism?", "summary": "Obesity is a marker for B3, but the absence of paraneoplastic features weakens this hypothesis. Net effect is weak_against B3."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of acanthocytosis or specific skin blistering patterns inconsistent with glucagonoma syndrome (B1.1) despite multisystem symptoms?", "summary": "Lack of acanthocytosis reduces support for B1.1, but multisystem symptoms maintain its possibility. This provides weak_against evidence."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of classic hypercortisolism features (e.g., striae, moon face) rule out ectopic ACTH syndrome (B1.2) as a dangerous alternative?", "summary": "Classic features are absent, but ectopic ACTH can present without them. This finding is neutral for B1.2."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the severe hyperglycemia (>500 mg/dL) with a painful rash more indicative of a neoplastic process than typical insulin resistance (B3)?", "summary": "Severe hyperglycemia and painful rash are atypical for B3, supporting neoplastic causes like B1.1. This is weak_against for B3."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B3", 0.481], ["B1", 0.459], ["B1.1", 0.241]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.55], ["B1.1", 0.465], ["B3", 0.14]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers (airway, respiratory, hemodynamic, neurologic, or hemorrhagic compromise) are present. Time-critical conditions like septic shock, STEMI, or meningitis are excluded by normal vital signs and lack of localized findings.", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading branch (B1.1) posterior (0.4647) < 0.75; dangerous alternatives (B1.1: danger=0.8, B1.2: danger=0.7) remain unresolved with posterior >= 0.12. Furthe